,title,link,text,language,family
0,Burji language - Wikipedia,https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Burji_language,"Burji language (alternate names: Bembala, Bambala, Daashi) is an Afro-Asiatic language spoken by the Burji people who reside in Ethiopia south of Lake Chamo. There are over 49,000 speakers in Ethiopia, and a further 36,900 speakers in Kenya.",Burji,Afro-Asiatic
1,Afroasiatic languages - Wikipedia,https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Afroasiatic_languages,"There are many well-attested Afroasiatic languages from antiquity that have since died or gone extinct, including Egyptian and the Semitic languages Akkadian, Biblical Hebrew, Phoenician, Amorite, and Ugaritic.",Burji,Afro-Asiatic
2,Burji / Daashi / Bembala / Bambala / ቡረጊ : The Burji Language ...Burji || Arba arba-burji cultural music | By burji people ...Burji.org — Preserving Burji HeritageBurji language - WikipediaAfroasiatic languages - WikipediaRepost from @eastafrican.music Burji tradition . Abbush did ...Afro-Asiatic,https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=s5EbmtVeF8c,"May 25, 2015 · Burji is an Afro-Asiatic language of the family's Cushitic branch, it's native speakers mainly inhabit Ethiopia and Kenya. Burji is one of the woredas in the Southern Nations, Nationalities, and Peoples' Region (SNNPR) of Ethiopia. Today, Burji communities thrive both in their ancestral homeland and across the global diaspora — connected by language, tradition, and a shared commitment to preserving their heritage for future generations. The Burji language belongs to the Cushitic branch of the Afro-Asiatic family. Burji language (alternate names: Bembala, Bambala, Daashi) is an Afro-Asiatic language spoken by the Burji people who reside in Ethiopia south of Lake Chamo. There are over 49,000 speakers in Ethiopia, and a further 36,900 speakers in Kenya. There are many well-attested Afroasiatic languages from antiquity that have since died or gone extinct, including Egyptian and the Semitic languages Akkadian, Biblical Hebrew, Phoenician, Amorite, and Ugaritic. Nov 29, 2020 · both afaan Oromo and Burji native language! Burji people are one of Ethiopia’s underrepresented people and culture. The burji people are from the southern region and live south of Lake Chamo. Their neighbors include the Gedeo and Konso ethnic groups as well as Borana Oromos ! They speak an Afro-asiatic language called burji. — Capturing the unique grammatical features and cultural concepts of Burji in translation can be challenging. Supports standard encoding and rendering for Ge’ez or Latin script. No major compatibility issues reported. The Burji people have a rich tradition of folk music and dance.",Burji,Afro-Asiatic
